The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
end = (u8 *)rsp_iov->iov_base + rsp_iov->iov_len;
if ((u8 *)ea > end - sizeof(*ea))
if ((u8 *)ea->ea_data + nlen + 1 + vlen > end)
```
Reason: The function validates each EA entry only against the end of the received iov buffer (`end`), but does not check that the entire EA region declared by `OutputBufferLength` fits within that buffer. If the server sends a truncated response with a large `OutputBufferLength` and few EA entries, the function may return success, and then `smb2_compound_op()` uses the oversized `OutputBufferLength` to `memcpy` beyond the allocation, causing an out-of-bounds read.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
ea_end = (u8 *)ea + outlen;
if (ea_end > (u8 *)rsp_iov->iov_base + rsp_iov->iov_len) return -EINVAL;
if ((u8 *)ea > ea_end - sizeof(*ea)) return -EINVAL;
```
Reason: The fix introduces `ea_end` (the end of the EA region based on `OutputBufferLength`) and validates that it does not exceed the actual iov buffer end. All subsequent bounds checks are changed to use `ea_end` instead of the iov end, ensuring that the function only succeeds when the entire declared EA data is fully contained within the received buffer. This prevents the out-of-bounds read in `smb2_compound_op()`.

